  "body": "Consider we have following markup:\n\n``` html\n<html>\n    <head></head>\n    <body>\n        <form action=\"url1\">\n            <div>\n        </form>\n        <form action=\"url2\">\n            <input type=\"hidden\" id=\"yo\" />\n        </form>\n    </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nit will be parsed as:\n\n``` html\n<html>\n    <head></head>\n    <body>\n      <form action=\"url1\">\n        <div>\n          <form action=\"url2\">\n            <input type=\"hidden\" id=\"yo\">\n          </form>\n        </div>\n      </form>\n    </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nThis is invalid markup, but it works just fine in DOM representation.\nNow if you serialize it back to HTML and re-parse it, e.g. doing:\n\n``` js\ndocument.body.innerHTML = document.body.innerHTML;\n```\n\nYou will get:\n\n``` html\n<html>\n    <head></head>\n    <body>\n        <form action=\"url1\">\n           <div>\n               <input type=\"hidden\" id=\"yo\">\n           </div>\n        </form>\n    </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nWhich completely changes semantics of document: input now has associated form with `url1`.\nAs a possible workaround we can add foster parenting-style algorithm for forms: we will just bailout any nested `form`s.\n",
